***************************************** important****************************
 if we have exactly one constructor the spring automatically uses it for Dependencies Injection we don't need Autowired
@Bean Annotation used only inside @Configuration Annotation when we manually create Objects


Because final fields in Java must be initialized exactly once — either:

at declaration, or
inside a constructor.

But field injection with @Autowired happens after the object is created.

So this will fail:

@Service
public class ExpenseService {

    @Autowired
    private final ExpenseRepository repository;
}

The compiler complains because:

final requires initialization during construction
but Spring tries to inject later using reflection
Why Constructor Injection Works with final
@Service
public class ExpenseService {

    private final ExpenseRepository repository;

    public ExpenseService(ExpenseRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Here:

constructor runs during object creation
final field gets initialized immediately
Java is satisfied

This is why constructor injection is the preferred modern approach in Spring Framework.

Timeline Difference
Field Injection (@Autowired on field)
Step 1

Java creates object:

new ExpenseService()
Step 2

Spring injects dependency afterward.

Problem:
final field was never initialized during construction.

Constructor Injection
Step 1

Spring calls:

new ExpenseService(repository)
Step 2

Constructor initializes final field immediately.

No issue.
